remember being sat by myself a lot  eating lunch alone  becoming more and more withdrawn  lizzy was only 12 years old when her  mental health deteriorated  the struggle with food i always say was  born out of my trouble with anxiety  because  i felt so out of control with everything  in my life and i can remember  making almost like lists in my head of  what i'd been eating that day and at one  point it was just that was the only  thing that gave me that sense of  security  she developed anorexia as a way of  coping with her anxiety and depression  by 15 her weight loss became so serious  lizzie was admitted to a psychiatric  unit for 10 weeks of life-saving  treatments  it was walking into hospital and seeing  other patients there and hearing the  alarms and seeing the locks on the doors  so for a 15 year old in a psychiatric  unit of a hospital that must be  terrifying  yeah it was i was like wow i must be  really sick to be here i remember  another patient there who had cuts all  down their arm  and there'd be alarms going off  where people had to be you know  withheld i called my parents and i  remember just crying to them please come  and get me i cannot stay here and  and that was just because the chaos  around me  more young people are being referred to  mental health services than ever before  in our service since 2019 we've seen an  increase in 140 percent the rate of  referrals in  dr dorothy newton and consultants in  eating disorders tell me social media  pressures around body image are helping  to drive the surge  i think that in many cases where a young  person develops an eating disorder it is  often multifactorial  but we have absolutely seen many young  people who are  developing eating disorders who feel  that they have been influenced  by online content and that has led to  for example anxiety about their body  shape  the pandemic has had a devastating  impact on young lives  adding to the pressure on already  stretched mental health services  are you confident mental health services  will be able to cope with this spike in  demand i think we'll cope we may have to  work differently but it's it's as busy  as it's been and it's as busy as i've  known in my career there is note that we  are in a different position to where we  were nationally about three years ago  numbers are up they're up across all age  groups and in all types of presentations  lizzie is now studying to be a doctor  one day  she hopes to use her own experiences  with other young people who need help  ashish joshi sky news west london
 

